WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.039 --> 00:00:03.630
Useful statuses let the department tell
the difference between a resource

2
00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:07.409
that can accept work and a resource that
is already doing something.

3
00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.130
Open the department menu and Custom
Statuses.

4
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:15.600
The page separates unit status sets,
personnel actions, and

5
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.079
personnel staffing levels.

6
00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:21.930
Unit status sets describe vehicles,
apparatus or field resources.

7
00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.079
Personnel actions describe what a person
is doing.

8
00:00:25.459 --> 00:00:28.329
Staffing describes whether that person is
available.

9
00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:33.150
A security patrol can be available while
On Patrol; a search volunteer

10
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:37.959
can be unavailable for deployment even if
the last activity says Standing By.

11
00:00:38.419 --> 00:00:41.700
Avoid trying to answer all those questions
with one label.

12
00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.580
The Chicago Fire names are simply our
synthetic demo roster.

13
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.069
These status concepts also serve mobile
response, search and rescue,

14
00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.130
security, wildland and industrial teams.

15
00:00:53.339 --> 00:00:55.389
Choose Add Unit Statuses.

16
00:00:55.859 --> 00:01:00.220
Resgrid offers starting templates for
ambulance, fire, law enforcement,

17
00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:04.830
search and rescue, general response,
disaster response, security,

18
00:01:04.979 --> 00:01:06.960
industrial fleets and logistics.

19
00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.259
Pick the one that fits the resource’s
actual lifecycle.

20
00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:16.509
We choose Search and Rescue, name the set
Training, S A R Field Team, and rename

21
00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:19.210
Searching to Searching assigned area.

22
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.890
Templates prefill options; they do not
save the set.

23
00:01:23.359 --> 00:01:27.189
You can review labels, colors and ordering
before clicking Add.

24
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.910
The saved set contains eight options.

25
00:01:30.309 --> 00:01:34.899
A security company can start from Security
and use On Patrol and Investigating.

26
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.219
An industrial team can use fleet and
equipment states such as Maintenance.

27
00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.969
A mobile response team can use a reviewed
response lifecycle.

28
00:01:43.349 --> 00:01:47.270
A wildland contractor can use staging and
response states appropriate

29
00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.930
to its crews and apparatus.

30
00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:51.070
Inspect an option’s Base Type.

31
00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.219
Searching has the meaning of actively
searching an

32
00:01:54.219 --> 00:01:56.449
assigned area, sector or grid.

33
00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:59.390
A clearer button label can preserve that
meaning.

34
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.880
Choosing None creates an informational
state without that system meaning.

35
00:02:04.349 --> 00:02:07.730
A label that says Ready should not be
assigned an Unavailable

36
00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:12.050
meaning, and an attractive green button is
not evidence of readiness.

37
00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.919
Review color contrast and use meaningful
text so members do

38
00:02:15.919 --> 00:02:17.939
not have to rely on color alone.

39
00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:22.779
This build exposed an option-edit issue:
changing the note and destination

40
00:02:22.779 --> 00:02:26.759
requirements and clicking Update returned
to the list, but reopening

41
00:02:26.759 --> 00:02:28.590
the option showed the old values.

42
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.329
We recorded that limitation and do not
call the edit successful.

43
00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:36.230
The next part demonstrates recording the
requirements during creation,

44
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.140
then checking the saved result.

45
00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:41.980
Use Start from Blank to build the
Training, Field Response set.

46
00:02:42.479 --> 00:02:44.839
Add Option opens the option dialog.

47
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:49.189
Our first option is Ready for assignment
with the Available base

48
00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:53.179
meaning, a dark green background, white
text and an optional note.

49
00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.500
Our second is Searching assigned area with
Searching meaning.

50
00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.610
We choose Calls as its detail type,
Required for its note

51
00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:04.039
type, and Require G P S.

52
00:03:04.449 --> 00:03:08.299
These controls express different
requirements: the detail supplies a

53
00:03:08.299 --> 00:03:13.230
destination such as an incident; the note
supplies context; the location

54
00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.409
requirement supplies a position where
supported by the client.

55
00:03:16.809 --> 00:03:20.390
A search team might record the assigned
area and team accountability.

56
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.379
A security patrol might record the site or
investigation reference.

57
00:03:24.839 --> 00:03:28.149
Keep mobile response notes within approved
operational-record

58
00:03:28.149 --> 00:03:32.490
procedures; a required note is not an
invitation to place sensitive

59
00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.830
clinical detail in every status update.

60
00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:39.050
Our third option is Unavailable,
maintenance, with the Unavailable

61
00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.800
meaning and a required note.

62
00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:44.539
This makes an equipment defect visible
without suggesting that the

63
00:03:44.539 --> 00:03:46.579
resource remains ready for dispatch.

64
00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:51.330
Set the order to zero, one and two so
Ready, Searching and

65
00:03:51.359 --> 00:03:53.140
Unavailable appear predictably.

66
00:03:53.619 --> 00:03:57.480
Adding an option to the dialog’s table is
still an unsaved change.

67
00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.859
Click Add on the main form to save the
entire set.

68
00:04:01.289 --> 00:04:04.550
The application returns to Custom Statuses
and shows the

69
00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:06.500
three-option Field Response set.

70
00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:09.000
Review that result before assigning it.

71
00:04:09.469 --> 00:04:13.379
For an industrial response team, this
pattern can keep defective equipment

72
00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.320
out of normal readiness assumptions.

73
00:04:15.689 --> 00:04:19.520
For wildland or search teams, it can
distinguish staging and active

74
00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.210
work from unavailable equipment.

75
00:04:21.629 --> 00:04:25.509
More options are useful only if members
can select them consistently.

76
00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:29.960
A new unit status set needs to be assigned
before a unit can use it.

77
00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:34.740
Open Types, find the Rescue unit type, and
choose Field Response

78
00:04:34.799 --> 00:04:37.360
under Actions, Custom Unit Statuses.

79
00:04:37.759 --> 00:04:38.740
Save the assignment.

80
00:04:39.259 --> 00:04:42.569
This changes the status choices for every
unit that uses the

81
00:04:42.569 --> 00:04:44.190
Rescue type in this department.

82
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.940
Squad Three is our example rescue unit.

83
00:04:47.399 --> 00:04:50.790
Reopen its status picker and check that
Ready for assignment,

84
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.589
Searching assigned area, and Unavailable
maintenance are present.

85
00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:58.180
For a security department, you might
assign a patrol status

86
00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.950
set to its patrol vehicle type.

87
00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:03.549
For an industrial team, separate equipment
types can use

88
00:05:03.549 --> 00:05:05.209
different operational choices.

89
00:05:05.689 --> 00:05:09.880
A type assignment changes the available
choices for all units of that type.

90
00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:13.279
Changing one unit's current status affects
that unit.

91
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.520
Select Searching assigned area.

92
00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:19.539
The destination picker now includes the
active training calls because

93
00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:21.460
Calls was recorded at creation.

94
00:05:21.889 --> 00:05:25.379
We choose the vehicle rescue exercise and
enter an accountability

95
00:05:25.379 --> 00:05:27.179
note for assigned area Alpha.

96
00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:28.410
Click Save.

97
00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:32.110
The unit list now displays Searching
assigned area with a

98
00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.849
new timestamp for Squad Three.

99
00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.370
That is the observed saved result.

100
00:05:36.679 --> 00:05:40.669
The web path shown here does not
demonstrate a device G P S capture

101
00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:44.069
or all mobile-client enforcement, so we do
not claim that it

102
00:05:44.069 --> 00:05:45.639
proves the location requirement.

103
00:05:46.089 --> 00:05:49.409
Test the member and unit apps your
department uses, including

104
00:05:49.409 --> 00:05:53.490
missing notes, unavailable location
permission and offline conditions.

105
00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:58.229
A saved status is a declaration of
activity; it does not prove the team

106
00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:03.040
completed a search, reached a subject, or
has an independently verified position.

107
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.410
Personnel actions and staffing have
separate template entry

108
00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.069
points on the original page.

109
00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:13.220
Before replacing either department-wide
set, review existing reset targets,

110
00:06:13.359 --> 00:06:17.090
suppression selections, dispatch
transitions and member training.

111
00:06:17.649 --> 00:06:21.519
For community paramedicine or mental
health response, use approved

112
00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:26.029
operational states such as En route to
visit or Contact made, with

113
00:06:26.029 --> 00:06:27.630
meanings appropriate to the work.

114
00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:31.769
For search and rescue, use Searching and
Subject Contact.

115
00:06:32.179 --> 00:06:35.799
For security, use On Patrol and
Investigating.

116
00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:40.450
For wildland contractors, use Staging,
Responding and Returning.

117
00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.630
For industrial teams, distinguish response
activity from equipment

118
00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:46.190
Maintenance and Unavailable.

119
00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.409
Your practice exercise is to create a
small set in a training department,

120
00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.380
explain each option’s meaning, assign it
to the intended unit type, save a

121
00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.659
real update and verify its display.

122
00:06:58.089 --> 00:07:02.330
Record any client behavior that still
needs an operational test and assign the

123
00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.359
next review to the responsible person.

